ility spanning NGS, gene expression profiling, tissue- and sometimes blood-based approaches\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Molecular testing is described as a broad category used synonymously with biomarker testing, evaluating tumor molecular characteristics (and in some cases blood) to guide diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment selection, including identification of DNA mutations, gene expression patterns, and protein expression/levels. [Data: Entities (1563)]\\n\\nThis umbrella explicitly includes multiple named approaches—tumor profiling, tumor sequencing, genomic testing, comprehensive genomic profiling, and next-generation sequencing—while noting that testing is typically performed on tumor tissue from a biopsy but may sometimes be performed using blood (“liquid biopsy”). [Data: Entities (1563)]\\n\\nGene expression profiling is specifically connected as an approach within molecular testing, measuring patterns of gene activity in cancer cells or tumor material (including tumor tissue and, in some cases, leukemia cells) to support disease classification and treatment decision-making. [Data: Relationships (2599); Entities (1624, 1563)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"A leukemia-focused branch centers on leukemia/AML cells as the analyte for somatic testing and prognostic stratification\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Leukemia cells are defined as malignant blood- or bone marrow–forming cells, with AML cells described as a specific type/subset within leukemia cells; these cells accumulate in marrow and are key targets for biomarker, genetic, and chromosomal testing. [Data: Entities (1703); Relationships (3044)]\\n\\nBiomarker and genetic testing is described for AML cells to characterize AML subtype, guide targeted treatment decisions, and estimate prognosis, and it is also commonly described as molecular testing, tumor profiling, gene expression profiling, or genomic testing—linking the leukemia branch to the broader molecular-testing terminology. [Data: Entitie